    "ls|LS16_S13_U_305": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 305\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH DECEMBER, 2017\nOut of School Students\n305. SHRI R. DHRUVA NARAYANA:\nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether 2.8 million children are out of school in the country as per the UNESCO  \nreport;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise; and\n(c)  whether  the  Government  has  taken  note  of  it  and  if  so,  the  details  of  the \ncomprehensive  measures  taken  by  the  Government  to  bring  these  children  to \nschool?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA)\n(a): Yes, Madam. As per UNESCO’s Global Education Monitoring Report, 2017-18,  \n2.8 million children were estimated to be out of school in India.\n(b): It has been informed by UNESCO that they collect data only at the national level. \nHence, State/UT-wise details are not available.\n(c): The Centrally Sponsored Scheme of Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) provides  \nassistance to State Governments and UTs to ensure free and compulsory quality  \nelementary education for all children. Universal enrolment is the first goal of SSA,  \nand hence all efforts are made to bring all children to schools. SSA has provided  \n2.06  lakh  primary  and  1.61  lakh  upper  primary  schools  for  ensuring  universal \nprovisioning of schooling facilities. Preference for opening of school is given to tribal  \nareas, and areas with high concentration of Scheduled Caste, Scheduled Tribe and  \nminority population. In addition, 3703 Kasturba Gandhi Balika Vidyalayas, which are  \nresidential schools for girls at upper primary level, have also been sanctioned to the  \nStates for improving access to marginalised girls particularly out of school, either  \ndrop out or never enrolled. As per Unified District Information System for Education  \n\n2015-16, the number of total children enrolled at elementary level has increased  \nfrom 15.1 crore in 1998-99 to 19.67 crore.\nUnder SSA, provisions have also been made for residential schools/hostels and  \ntransportation/escort facility for children living in sparsely populated areas, children  \nliving in areas where schools cannot be opened due to unavailability of land and  \nchildren in need of care and protection. Special training is also provided to all such  \nchildren who are school dropouts and long absentees and they are enrolled in “back  \nto school” camps.\nAdditionally,  other  strategies  adopted  under  SSA such  as  strengthening  school \ninfrastructure, improving pupil-teacher ratios, providing incentives like free textbooks,  \nuniforms for eligible category of children and mid-day meal in schools have proved to \nbe useful in increasing the enrolment of children in elementary schools.\n*******",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_306": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 306 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18th DECEMBER 2017 \nAGRAHAYANA 27, 1939 (SAKA)  \n \nBiannual NEET and JEE. \n \n306.      SHRI BHAGWANT MANN: \n           \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to conduct medical and engineering \n admission exams, National Eligibility cum Entrance Test (NEET) and Joint \n Entrance Examination (JEE) twice a year to reduce pressure on students; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the way students are likely to be benefited by the said pattern? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n \n(a) to (c): The Government has decided to setup a National Testing Agency (NTA) \nas a Society registered under the Indian Societies Registration Act, 1860, and as an \nautonomous and self -sustained premier testing organization to conduct all entrance \nexaminations for higher educational institutions.  The features for the said examinations \nare (i) the NTA would initially conduct those entrance examinations which are curren tly \nbeing conducted by the CBSE; (ii) Other examinations will be taken up gradually after \nNTA is fully geared up; (iii) The entrance examinations will be conducted in online mode \nat least twice a year, thereby giving adequate opportunity to can didates to bring out \ntheir best; and (iv) In order to serve the requirements of the ru ral students, it would \nlocate the centres at sub -district/district level and as far as possible would undertake \nhands-on training to the students. \n*** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_307": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 307 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \n \nLABOUR CONFERENCE \n307. SHRI G. HARI: \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a)whether the Government has finalised the agenda for the 47th Indian \nLabour Conference;  \n(b)if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)whether providing social security cover for the entire workforce may \nbe the central theme of the 47th Indian Labour Conference and if  so, \nthe details thereof;  \n(d)whether the Government has received comments/suggestions from all \nstakeholders for finalising the agenda of Indian Labour Conference; \nand  \n(e)if so, the details thereof?  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a)  to (c): Yes, Madam. The agenda of the 47 th Indian Labour \nConference was decided in the 48 th Session of Standing Labour \nCommittee held on 11 th May, 2017 in New Delhi and the details of \nagenda are as under: \n \n i.     Social Security for all. \n ii.    Labour law amendment, reforms and codification \n iii.   Employment Generation with special focus on women \n iv.   Transition to formal employment.  \n  \n(d) & (e):  The agenda of Indian Labour Conference is finalised by the \ntripartite stakeholders after a thorough deliberations in Standing Labour \nCommittee as indicated above.  \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_308": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 308 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \n \nGST LITERACY UNDER PMKVY \n \n \n308. SHRI RAVNEET SINGH \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP \nbe pleased to state : \n  \n(a) whether any support has been provided under the  Pradhan  Mantri Kaushal \nVikas Yojana (PMKVY) to create a GST literate workf orce that can assist smaller \nenterprises for their GST filings and if so, the details thereof; and \n   \n(b) the details of the curriculum of the courses, n umber of people trained and \nduration of the course, State-wise ? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI ANANTKUMAR HEGDE) \n \n(a) and (b) Ministry of Skill Development and Entre preneurship, in consultation \nwith Department of Revenue, Central Board  of Excis e & Custom (CBEC), and \nNational Academy of Customs, Indirect Taxes & Narco tics (NACIN) under  Ministry \nof Finance, had finalized a roadmap to execute a tr aining program for GST under \nMSDE’s flagship program Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vika s Yojana (PMKVY). The \ncandidates trained under this program provide suppo rt to small and medium \nenterprises/entrepreneurs for filing their returns under GST. \n \nTo execute this training, Banking Financial Service s and Insurance Sector \nSkill Council (BFSI SSC) under the supervision of M SDE, developed a 140 hour \n(including 40 hours of digital literacy) course cal led the GST Accounts Assistant \nTraining. The job role is a level 4 course as per N ational Skill Qualification \nFramework (NSQF) and is duly validated by industry experts. Under PMKVY (2016-\n20), 4375 candidates have been trained under GST. The State-wise details are given \nat Annexure . \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nState-wise number of candidates trained under GST A ccounts Assistant job \nrole under PMKVY 2016-20: \n \nS. No. State Name Total Trained \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 150 \n2.  Assam 150 \n3.  Bihar 449 \n4.  Chhattisgarh 89 \n5.  Delhi 150 \n6.  Gujarat 0 \n7.  Haryana 179 \n8.  Jammu and Kashmir 84 \n9.  Jharkhand 0 \n10.  Karnataka 436 \n11.  Kerala 150 \n12.  Madhya Pradesh 277 \n13.  Maharashtra 45 \n14.  Odisha 283 \n15.  Punjab 60 \n16.  Rajasthan 141 \n17.  Tamil Nadu 0 \n18.  Telangana 120 \n19.  Tripura 90 \n20.  Uttar Pradesh 1222 \n21.  Uttarakhand 0 \n22.  West Bengal 300 \nTotal  4375  \n \n****** \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_309": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 309\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH DECEMBER, 2017\nDifferently Abled Children\n309. SHRI B. SENGUTTUVAN:\nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is true that the  attendance of the differently abled children in the age  \ngroup of 5-19 years at school has gone up and if so, the details thereof; and\n(b) the percentage of the differently abled children attending schools in the country  \nduring the last three years, State/UT-wise?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA)\n(a): Attendance record of the differently abled children is not maintained Centrally.  \nHowever,  enrolment of children with  special  needs  (CWSN) at elementary level \n(Class I to VIII) has decreased from 25.03 lakh in 2013-14 to 22.86 lakh in 2015-16.  \nSimilarly, at the secondary (Class IX – X) and higher secondary level (Class XI – XII)  \nthe number of CWSN has gone down from 2.88 lakh in 2013-14 to 2.79 lakh in 2015-\n16.\n(b): State/UT-wise details of the percentage of enrolment of differently abled children  \nattending schools in the country during the last three years as per Unified District  \nInformation System for Education are at Annexure-I & II.\n*******\n\nANNEXURE-I\nANNEXURE  REFERRED  TO  IN  REPLY  TO  PART  (b)  OF  LOK  SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 309, ASKED BY SHRI B. SENGUTTUVAN TO BE \nANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 REGARDING “DIFFERENTY ABLED CHILDREN”\nStatement showing percentage of enrolment of CWSN at primary and upper primary level\nSource: Unified District Information System for Education\nANNEXURE-II\nSl \nNo State/UT Primary Level Upper Primary Level\n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16\n1 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 1.08 0.86 1.08 1.02 0.97 0.87\n2 Andhra Pradesh 1.81 1.73 1.81 1.03 1.07 1.07\n3 Arun