    "ls|LS16_S13_U_328": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 328 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \n \nRecommendations on New Education Policy \n \n328.  KUMARI  SUSHMITA DEV: \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has accepted any of the recommendations made  by  the  T.S.R.  \nSubramanian Committee in the New Education Policy -2016 and if so, the details  of  the \nrecommendations that were accepted and implemented; \n \n(b) whether the Government has not accepted any of the recommendations and if so, the reasons \ntherefor; \n \n(c) the reasons for not publishing the complete report of  the  2016  draft  in  the public domain; \nand  \n \n(d)  whether  the  Government  intends to Publish the complete report and if so, the details for the \nestimated time frame for  the  same  and  if  not,  the  reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(Dr. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n \n(a) to (d) : The Government is in the process of formulating a New Education Policy  for which \ninitially, it had constituted a Committee for Evolution of the New Education Policy  in October \n2015 under the C hairmanship of Shri T.S.R. Subramanian, former Cabinet Secretar y to the \nGovernment of India  which submitted its report in May, 2016. The Report of the Committee is \navailable on the website of NUEPA , which was the Committee’s Secretariat , and can be accessed \nat http://www.nuepa.org/New/download/NEP2016/ReportNEP.pdf. \nAfter perusal of the report of the Committee and the based on reports of various consultations as \nwell as views and comments received, the Ministry prepared “Some Inputs for the D raft National \nEducation Policy, 2016 ” which is  available on the MHRD website at \nhttp://mhrd.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/Inputs_Draft_NEP_2016.pdf. Both these reports \nhave been treated as inputs for education policy formulation.  \nThe exercise of preparing a New Education Policy is still ongoing as the Government has recently \nconstituted a Co mmittee for preparation of the D raft National Education Policy under the \nChairmanship of Dr. K. Kasturirangan, which is due to submit its report soon. \n \n********** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_329": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.329  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18th DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nPETROCHEMICAL INVESTORS CONCLAVE 2017 \n \n 329: SHRI J.C. DIVAKAR REDDY: \n \nपैट्रोल ियम और प्राकृ तिक गैस मंत्री \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has held Petrochemical Investors Conclave 2017 and also \nHydrocarbon related conference in Visakhapatnam, Andhra Pradesh recently; and   \n(b) if so, the details thereof and issues discussed therein and the outcome thereof?  \n \nANSWER \nपैट्रोलियम और प्राक ृ लिक गैस मंत्री (श्री धमेन्द्र प्रधान) \nMINISTER OF PETROELUM & NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) & (b): Petrochemical Investors Conclave 2017 was held at Bhubaneshwar on \n16.11.2017.   Over 750 delegates attended the Conclave.  During the conclave, three \nMoUs were signed, each with I nstitute of Chemical Technology (I CT), Mumbai, MCPI \nPrivate Ltd, West Bengal  and Odisha Industrial Infrastructure Development Corporation \n(IDCO), Odisha by Indian Oil Corporation Ltd. \nFurther,    21st Refinery Technology Meet was organised at Visakhapatnam in April, 2017 \nwith the theme “Refining to Petrochemicals -The way Ahead”. Large number of \ndelegates/speakers/ senior executives from Oil Industry around the world participated in \nthe Meet. 74 number s of technical papers, including 47 papers from Global leaders in \nRefining and Petrochemical Technology were presented. The Meet provided opportunity \nfor exchange of latest technological advances and best practices followed worldwide. \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_330": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 330 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \n \nRecognition of CA Course \n \n330. SHRI JAYADEV GALLA: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware that the Institute of Chartered Accountants (ICA) \nof India have been requesting to recognize Chartered Accountant(CA) Intermediate as \nequivalent to graduation and CA (Final) equivalent as post -graduation and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(b)  whether the Association of Indian Universities has recognized this and many \nuniversities/Indian Institutes of Management(IIMs) and Indian Institutes of \nTechnology(IITs) have also recognized this and if so, the details thereof; and \n(c)  the reasons for acceding to their request? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n \n(a):  The Institute of Chartered Accountants (ICA) of India has approached the Ministry for \nobtaining recognition of CA qualification as equivalent to P ost Graduation degree in \nCommerce and Accountancy offered by Universities/ Colleges recognized by University \nGrants Commission. \n(b) & (c): Association of Indian Universities (AIU) in the year 1984 , based on the request \nfrom the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India, New Delhi  has resolved that the \nGraduates having passed their final examination of the Institute of Chartered Accountants of \nIndia, New Delhi be treated to have completed a Postgr aduate Degree in Commerce or allied \ndisciplines for purpose of registration to Ph.D.  However, the Government has not issued any \nkind of equivalence to CA qualification and neither endorsed the decision of AIU. Further, it \nis mentioned that the Universities / Higher Educational Institutions are independent to \nrecognize degrees for the purpose of pursuing higher education. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_331": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.331 \nANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \n \nEMPLOYMENT THROUGH TOURISM \n \n331.  DR. SHASHI THAROOR:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has announced to cr eate 100 million \njobs through tourism and attract 40 million foreign tourists \nannually in the next five years;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the action plan chalked out \nby the Government to ensure the realization of these goals; \nand  \n(c)  the detailed measures taken/being taken by the Government to \nensure that the inflow of tourists do not reduce owing to the \nfear caused by incidents of attacks on tourists reported in \nvarious parts of the country in the recent past? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI K. J. ALPHONS) \n \n(a): Being a highly labour intensive activity, the tourism sector is \nan important source of employment in the country. Recognizing the \npotential of tourism sector in economic growth as well as in job \ncreation, the Ministry of Tourism facilitates creation of skilled/semi -\nskilled manpower to service the tourism industry and in turn provide \nemployment to a wide spectrum of job seekers from the unskilled to \nthe specialized. The Ministry of Tourism aims at c reating world \nclass infrastructure facilities at airports, railway stations, tourist \ndestinations, World Heritage Sites and Archaeological Survey of \nIndia (ASI) protected monuments to attract more number of tourists.  \n \n\n(b) and (c):  Ministry of Tourism has  taken various effective \nmeasures to create jobs, attract more tourists and ensure safety \nand security, which inter-alia, include the following: \n \ni) Launching the Swadesh Darshan Scheme for integrated \ndevelopment of theme based tourist circuits. \nii) Launching the National Mission on Pilgrimage \nRejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage Augmentation Drive \n(PRASHAD) to beautify and improve amenities and \ninfrastructure at pilgrimage sites. \niii) Promoting India as a holistic tourism destination in the \ndomestic and international markets. \niv) Launching e-Visa for citizens of 163 countries. \nv) Developing and promoting ‘Niche Tourism’ products. \nvi) Generating sufficient manpower to meet the \nrequirements of the tourism and hospitality industry. \nvii) Launching 24x7 toll free Multi-Lingual Tourist Helpline. \nviii) Providing Central Financial Assistance (CFA) to State \nGovernments/Union Territory Administrations for fairs, \nfestivals and tourism related events. \nix) Launch the Code of Conduct for adoption by the Indian \nTravel and Tourism Industry for Safe and Honourable \nTourism. \nx) Issue of advisories from time to time to various State \nGovernments and UT Administrations to ensure safety & \nsecurity of tourists. \n \nIn addition, some State Governments/UT Administrations have \ndeployed Tourist Police for the safety of tourists. \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_333": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.333  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18th DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nIMPORT OF ARTIFICIAL DIAMONDS FROM CHINA \n \n 333.  SHRI G.M. SIDDESHWARA \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाͨणÏय एवं उɮयोग मंğी ) be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is aware that artificial diamonds are being imported from \nChina in large numbers which look like real ones but have no resale value; \n(b) if so, the reaction of the Government thereto; \n(c) whether the Government is also aware that Indian consumers are being cheated due \nto lack of identification of genuineness of imported diamonds from China; and  \n(d) if so, the steps taken by Government to save the Indian consumers from being \ncheated? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jh lh- vkj- pkS/kjh½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY  \n (SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY)  \n \n \n \n(a) to (d): Artificial diamonds are also known as “man-made”/“synthetic”/ “lab-created”/ “lab-\ngrown” diamonds. These are artificially created stones that are made in specialized laboratory \nconditions. These diamonds have the same physical and chemical properties and are identical \nto diamonds of natural origin.  \n \nWith growth in this specialised sector, the diamond industry brought to the notice of the \nGovernment the need for creation of a separate Harmonised Systems Code (HS Code) for \nSynthetic Diamonds and synthetic stones. Accordingly, in the interest of the consumers, two \nseparate codes were created in the Finance Bill, 2016. The new codes created are 7104 90 10 \nfor Synthetic Diamonds & 7104 90 90 for synthetic stones, thereby establishing a clear \nclassification differentiation from natural Diamonds which fall under HS Code Heading: \n7102.  \n \n \nImport data of Diamonds under Exim Code 7102 (Diamonds, whether or not worked, but not \nmounted or set) and 7104 90 10 (Laboratory created or laboratory grown or manmade or \ncultured or synthetic diamonds) from China vis-a-vis total imports is as under: \n \n \n\n \n \n \nTable:1 \n \nImport of diamonds (HS code 7102) \n \nValues in US $ Million \n \nYear Total Imports   \nImports from \nChina \n 2014-15 20,495.36  1. 30 \n 2015-16 16,865.96  6.21 \n 2016-17 19,332.14  1.31 \n 2017-18 (APR TO SEP) 15,091.34*  0.17* \n \n    * provisional. \n \nTable: 2 \n \nImport under HS Code 71049010 (Lab grown Diamonds) \nValues in US $ Mill \nYear \nTotal \nImports   Imports from China \n2017-18 (APR TO SEP) 160.33* 1.35* \n \n           * provisional. \n \nFrom the above, it may be seen that imports from China is miniscule compared to our total \nimport.  \n \n*** \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_334": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  No. 334 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 18th DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nGLOBAL ENERGY COMPANY RANKINGS \n \n334.  SHRI DIBYENDU ADHIKARI:  \nपेट्रोलियम और प्राकृलिक गैस मंत्री \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether  the Indian Energy Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs) have reportedly \nlost their rankings in the Global Energy Company (GEC) Rankings as compared to \nthe Indian Multinational Agencies; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof during the last three years along with th e financial \nstatements of the Indian companies therein; \n(c) the details of S&P Global Platts Top 250 GEC rankings in terms of audited \nfinancial performance and the debt position of all Indian energy companies listed in \nthe said rankings; and  \n(d) the detai ls of merger/acquisition proposals of the energy PSUs of the country at \npresent?  \n \nANSWER \n \nपेट्रोलियम और प्राकृलिक गैस मंत्री (श्री धमेन्द्र प्रधान) \nMINISTER OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS \n \n (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (c): As far as oil and gas Public  Sector Undertakings under the administrative \njurisdiction of Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas are concerned, they have \nimproved their Global Energy Company Ranking.  The details of their rankings \nduring the last three years are at Annexure -I.  Their financial statements and the \ndebt position for the last three years are at Annexure-II. \n \n(d): As far as Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas is concerned, the Cabinet \nCommittee on Economic Affairs (CCEA) has given in -principle approval for strategic \nsale of the Government of India’s existing 51.11% of total paid up equity \nshareholding in Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited (HPCL) to Oil and Natural \nGas Corporation (ONGC). \n***** \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nAnnexure-I referred to in parts (a) to (c) of the Lok Sabha Un starred Question \nNo.334 for 18.12.2017 regarding “Global Energy Company Ranking s” asked by \nShri Dibyendu Adhikari. \nGlobal Energy Ranking s of Indian Oil and Gas PSUs under the administrative \njurisdiction of Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas \n \nSl.No. Name of PSU 2015 2016 2017 \n1. Indian Oil Corporation Limited \n(IOCL) \n66 14 7 \n2 Oil and Natural Gas Corporation \nLimited (ONGC) \n17 20 11 \n3 Bharat Petroleum Corporation \nLimited (BPCL) \n59 35 27 \n4 Hindustan Petroleum Corporation \nLimited (HPCL) \n133 48 48 \n5. GAIL (India) Limited 120 132 106 \n6 Oil India Limited (OIL) 222 201 232 \n \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-II \nAnnexure-II referred to in parts (a) to (c) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo.334 for 18.12.2017 regarding “Global Energy Company Ranking s” asked by \nShri Dibyendu Adhikari. \nThe financial performance and debt position of Indian Oil and Gas PSUs for \nthe last 3 years \n(Rs. in crore) \nName of \nthe PSU \nParticulars 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \n \nIOCL \n \nRevenue from \nOperation (Gross) \n4,67,932 4,06,828 4,45,373 \nProfit after Tax 5,273 11,242 19,106 \nEquity 67,970 88,135 99,729 \nBorrowings 55,248 52,880 54,820 \n \nONGC \n \nRevenue from \nOperations \n1,66,389 1,35,664 1,42,149 \nProfit After Tax (PAT) 18,334 12,875 20,498 \nDebt/Equity Ratio 0.27 0.20 0.21 \nBPCL Total Revenue 2,40,287 1,91,315 2,44,648 \nProfit After Tax 5,085 7,432 8,039 \nTotal Borrowing 13,098 15,976 23,159 \nDebt/Equity Ratio 0.58 0.58 0.78 \nHPCL Gross Sales of Product 2,17,061 1,97,438 2,13,489 \nPBDIT 6,832 9,083 12,092 \nProfit After Tax 2,733 3,726 6,209 \nNetworth as on 31st \nMarch \n16,022 17,970 20,347 \nBorrowings as on 31st \nMarch \n \n20,335 21,167 21,250 \nGAIL Networth 28,888 30,699 32,350 \nProfit After Tax 3,039 2,226 3,503 \nGross Margin (EBIDTA) 5,620 5,172 7,287 \nReserves & Surplus 27,851 29,316 36,458 \nEarnings Per Share 24 18 21 \nDebt/Equity Ratio \n \n0.33 0.26 0.16 \nOIL Networth 21,497.59 22,802.26 23,201.74 \nRevenue 11,019.86 11,158.63 11,191.07 \nProfit after Tax 2,510.20 2,301.67 1,548.68 \nDebt 8,341.08 9,132.84 8,947.53 \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_335": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.335 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \n \nBudget Cuts for Navodaya Vidyalaya Samiti \n \n†335. SHRI NAGAR RODMAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the instances wherein the budget provisions of schools run by Navodaya  \nVidyalaya Samiti have been modified during the last five years; and \n \n(b) the details thereof State/UT-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n \n \n(a) The budget provisions for the schools run by Navodaya  Vidyalaya Samiti (NVS) \nhave been increasing over the years. The funds provided to NVS as grants in aid \nduring the last five years is as follows: \n \n         (Rs.in Crores) \nYear Amount  \n2012-13 1721.40 \n2013-14 1746.05 \n2014-15 2012.93 \n2015-16 2285.14 \n2016-17 2619.57 \n \n \n(b) NVS further allocates funds to the  Jawahar Navodaya Vidyalayas through  its \neight Regional offices as per approved norms of the Samiti and are not allocated \nState/UT wise. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_336": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n  \nLOK SABHA \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.336 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18th December, 2017 \n \nFACILITIES IN MADARSAS \n†336. SHRI TAMRADHWAJ SAHU: \n  \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government is sensitive towards the education of minority Muslim \ncommunity and if so, the details of number of Madarsas set up during the last three years, \nState/UT-wise; \n(b) the details of facilities provided to students studying in these Madarsas, State/UT-wise; \n(c) the number of staff working along with the number of students enrolled in these \nMadarsas; and \n(d) the facilities provided for the staff and the amount of funds spent on their salaries and \nperks? \n  \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n \n (a)&(b) Yes, Madam. The Ministry of Human Resource Develop ment is implementing a \ncentrally sponsored scheme namely Scheme for Providing Quality Education in Madarsas \n(SPQEM) which provides financial assistance to encourage traditional institutions like Madarsas \nand Maktabs to introduce modern education through su bjects such as science, mathematics, \nsocial studies, Hindi and English in their curriculum through support for a maximum of three \nteachers and for books, teaching learning material and computer labs. The details of number of \nMadrasa/Maktabs set -up in State s/UTs are not centrally maintained. State -wise details of \nfacilities provided i.e teachers' salary, teacher training, library books, science and maths kits, \ncomputer labs and science labs under the scheme during 2014 -15, 2015-16 and 2016 -17 are at \nAnnexure A, Annexure B and Annexure C respectively. \n(c)&(d) Under the SPQEM scheme,  financial assistance is given to the madarsas through the \nstates for salary of  full time Graduate teachers @ Rs.6000/ -p.m. and Post Graduate/B.Ed. \nteachers @Rs. 12000/-p.m. The number of staff working alongwith number of students enrolled \nin these Madrasas  is not maintained Centrally as the Scheme does not have any provision for \nappointment of Teachers. \n**** \n\nANNEXURE AS REFERRED  TO IN THE REPLY OF P ARTS (A) &  (B) OF THE LOK \nSABHA UNSTARRED QUES TION NO. 336 FOR 18. 12.2017 REGARDING FACILITIES IN \nMADARSAS ASKED BY SHRI TAMRADHWAJ SAHU.  \nAnnexure A \nComponent-wise Expenditure under SPQEM for the year 2014-15. \nAmount in Lakhs \nS. \nNo. \nStates Teachers \nsalary  \nTeacher \nTraining \nLib.Books \n(One \nTime) \nScience & \nMaths Kit \nComputer \nlab/Sci. Lab \n(One Time) \nDev. Of \nMadarsa \nBoard \nTotal \n1 Uttar Pradesh 8229.16   236.25 70.88 49.5   8585.8 \n2 Uttarakhand 310.11 0.245 10.675 1.575 1.65   324.26 \n3 Madhya pradesh 1172.52   85.75 14.55 44.85 5.00 1322.67 \n4 Karnataka 44.28   11.25 3.375 22.5   81.405 \n5 West Bengal 244.44 3.87 34.4 12.9 8.00   303.61 \n6 Tripura 144.36       1.00   145.36 \n7 Bihar 26.28           26.28 \n  Total 10171.15 4.115 337.40 103.28 115.00 5.00 10789.00 \n  \nAnnexure B \n \nAnnexure C \n  \n**** \n \n \n \nComponent-wise Expenditure under SPQEM for the year 2015-16. \nAmount in Lakhs \nS. \nNo. \nStates Teacher \nsalary \nTeacher \nTraining/ \nTeaching \nLrng \nmaterial \nLib. \nBooks \nScience \n& \nMaths \nKit \nOne Time \ngrant for \ncomputer \n/ Science \nLab \nComputer lab \n(Annual) \nDev. of \nMadarsa \nBoard \nTotal \n1 Chattisgarh 347.76 0.5025 12.775 2.025 6.5 1.5 5.00 364.1075 \n2 Madhya Pradesh 2294.64 57.5 79.32 17.24 56 25.65 2.50 2532.85 \n3 West Bengal 438.95 12.25   3.67 6.5     461.37 \n4 Uttarakhand 869.21 1.2875 16.3 2.6 1.5 1.65   922.4975 \n5 Tripura 296.64 2.9025 3.225   1.00 1.00 2.50 305.2675 \n6 Uttar Pradesh 23819.83 32.2575 544.5 163.35 125     23101.31 \n7 Bihar 608.58 281.75   84.525   563.5 5.00 1543.36 \n8 Jharkhand 204.48             204.48 \n  Total 27312.46 388.45 656.12 273.41 196.5 593.3 15.00 29435.24 \nComponent-wise Expenditure  under SPQEM for the year 2016-17. \nAmount in Lakhs \nS. \nNo. \nStates Teacher \nsalary \nTeacher \nTraining/ \nTeaching \nLearning \nmaterial \nLib. \nBooks \n(One \nTime) \nLib. \nBook \nScience \n& \nMaths \nKit \nOne Time \ngrant for \ncomputer \n/ Science \nLab \nComputer \n lab \n(Annual) \nDev. of \nMadarsa \nBoard \nTotal \n1 Chhattisgarh 637.92 0.5025 12.3 5.975 2.025 6.5 2.75 10.00 677.97 \n2 Madhya Pradesh 1363.12   48.5 42.075 14.55 42.5 13.3 5.00 1529.05 \n3 Uttar Pradesh 8089.35               8089.35 \n4 Uttarakhand 382.52 0.2175 3.00 6.35 0.97   0.87   393.93 \n  Total 10472.91 0.72 63.8 54.4 17.55 49.00 16.92 15.00 10690.40 \n\nभारत सरकार \nमानव संसाधन ववकास मंत्रालय \nस कू ल क्षा ा  स साषा स ा ववगा   \nलोक सभा \nअ ासांकक  प्रश् न संख् या: 336 \nउत्  स ेननन क   ासख : 18.12.2017 \n27 अग्रहायण, 1939 (्क) \n \nमदरसों में सुविधाएं \n336. श्री ताम्रध्िज साह ूः \n \nक्या मानव संसाधन ववकास मंत्री यह ब ानन क  कृपा कसें न ककिः \n \n(क) क्या ससकास अल्पसंख्यक मुसलमान समुेाय क  क्षा ा कन प्रत  संवनेन्ील है  स यदे \nहां,  ो वव    ीन वर्षों कन ेौसान सथावप  मेससों क  संख्या का साज्य /संघ साज्यषा नत्र-वास \nब्यौसा क्या है; \n( ) इन मेससों में पढ़ सहन ववद्यार्थियों को  मुहैया कसाई  ई सुववधाओं का साज्य/संघ \nसाज्यषा नत्र-वास ब्यौसा क्या है; \n( ) इन मेससों में कायिस  सटाफ क  संख्या  था पंजीकृ  ववद्यार्थियों क  संख्या क्या है;  स \n(घ) सटाफ को प्रेत्  सुववधाएं क्या हैं  स उनकन वन न एवं गत् ों पस व्यय धनसाक् कक नी \nहै? \nउत्तर \nमानि संसाधन विकास मंत्रालय में राज् य मंत्री \n (श्री उपेन्द्   कुहिाहा) \n(क) और (ख): जी, हााँ| मानव संसाधन ववकास मंत्रालय मेससों में  ुणवत् ा युक्   क्षा ा प्रेान कसनन \nहन ु योजना (एसपीक्यू ईएम) नामक एक क ें द्रखय प्रायोजज  योजना का कायािन्वयन कस सहा है| यह \nयोजना पसम्पसा   संसथाओं जैसन मेससों  स मख् बों को प्रोत्सादह  कसनन, अर्धक म  ीन क्षा कों \n स पुस कों क  सहाय ा कन माध्यम सन अपनन पाठ्यक्रम में ववज्ञान,  णण , सामाजजक ववज्ञान, दहंेख \n स अंग्रनज़ी ववर्षयों को ्ाकमल कस आधुतनक क्षा ा क  ्ुरुआ  कसनन, क्षा ण अर्ध म सामग्री  स \nकंप्यू टस प्रयो ्ालाओं को ववत् ीय सहाय ा प्रेान कस ी है| साज्यों/संघ साज्य षा नत्रों में सथावप  \nमेससों/मख् बों क  संख्या कन ब्यौसन क ें द्रखय रूप सन नहखं स न जा न हैं| इस योजना कन  ह  वर्षि 2014-15, \n2015-16  स 2016-17 कन ेौसान उपलब्ध कसवाई  ई सुववधाओं जैसन क्षा कों का वन न, क्षा क \nप्रक्षा ण, पुस कालय पुस कें , ववज्ञान  स  णण  ककट, कंप्यू टस प्रयो ्ाला  स ववज्ञान \nप्रयो ्ालाओं कन साज्यवास ब्यौसन क्रम्िः संलग्नक ‘क’, संलग्नक ‘ ’  स संलग्नक ‘ ’ पस देए  यन हैं|  \n \n(ग) और (घ): एसपीक्यू ईएम योजना कन  ह , साज्यों कन माध्यम सन मेससों कन कलए पू णिकाकलक सना क \nक्षा कों कन कलए 6000/- रूपयन प्रत माह  स सना कोत् स/बी.एड क्षा कों कन कलए 12,000/- प्रत माह क  \nेस सन वन न कन कलए ववत् ीय सहाय ा ेख जा ी है| इन मेससों में कायिस  सटाफ क  संख्या कन साथ-साथ \nनामांकक  छात्रों क  संख्या का क ें द्रखय रूप सन स -स ाव नहखं ककया जा ा क्योंकक योजना में क्षा कों क  \nतनयुजक्  हन ु कोई प्रावधान नहखं है|   \n***** \n\n“मदरसों में सुविधाओं’’ के संबंध में माननीय संसद सदस् य श्री ताम्रध् िज साह  द्िारा ददनांक 18.12.2017 को प छे \nजाने िाले लोक सभा अतारांककत प्रश् न सं् या 336 के  भाग (क) और (ख) के  उत् तर में उ्लिलिखत संलग्नक \nसंलग् नक क \n 2014-15 कन कलए एसपीक् यू ईएम कन   ह  घटक-वास व् यय                                                    रू. लाख में  \nक्र.सं. साज् य क्षा क वन न क्षा ण \nप्रक्षा ण \nपुस  कालय \nपुस  क (एक \nबास) \nववज्ञान एवं \n णण  क ट \nकंप् यू टस \nलैब/ववज्ञान \n(एक बास) \nमद्रास \nबोडि का \nववकास \nकुल \n1 उत् स प्रेन् 8229.16   236.25 70.88 49.5   8585.8 \n2 उत् सा ंड 310.11 0.245 10.675 1.575 1.65   324.26 \n3 मध्य प्रेन् 1172.52   85.75 14.55 44.85 5.00 1322.67 \n4 कनािटक 44.28   11.25 3.375 22.5   81.405 \n5 पजश्िम बं ाल 244.44 3.87 34.4 12.9 8.00   303.61 \n6 त्रत्रपुसा 144.36       1.00   145.36 \n7 त्रबहास 26.28           26.28 \n  कुल 10171.15 4.115 337.40 103.28 115.00 5.00 10789 \n \nसंलग् नक ख \n 2015-16 कन कलए एसपीक् यू ईएम कन   ह  घटक-वास व् यय                                       