    "ls|LS16_S13_U_352": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.352 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nUNEMPLOYMENT \n \n352.    SHRI RAJESH KUMAR DIWAKER: \nSHRI DEEPENDER SINGH HOODA: \nSHRI M.B. RAJESH: \nSHRI NISHIKANT DUBEY: \nSHRI MANSHANKAR NINAMA: \nSHRI MALLIKARJUN KHARGE: \nSHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL: \nSHRI VENKATESH BABU T.G.: \nSHRI OM PRAKASH YADAV: \nSHRI P. KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether unemployment is on the rise in the country during the last one year since \nNovember, 2016 and is reportedly at an all time high at present and if so, the details \nthereof and the reasons threrefor along with the employment generated both in \norganised and unorganised sectors during the last three years and the current year, \nState/UT-wise; \n \n(b) the schemes/programmes being implemented by the Government for eradication \nof unemployment in the country and the achievements thereof during the said period, \nyear-wise and State/UT-wise including Maharashtra;  \n \n(c) whether any evaluation has been done by the Government to ascertain \nemployment and unemployment situation in the country and if so, the details and the \nfindings thereof along with the follow-up action taken/being taken thereon; and \n \n(d) the other step s including identification of certain Core Sectors that can create \nhigh employment growth taken/being taken by the Government to remove \nunemployment/create more employment opportunities in the country?  \n \n \n \n\nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a): As per the result of surveys on employment and unemployment conducted by \nLabour Bureau, Ministry of Labour and Employment, the estimated unemployment rate \nand Workers Population Ratio (WPR) for persons aged 15 years and above on usual \nstatus basis in the country is given below:                                         \n \n \n \n   \nFindings of Anuual Surveys are not available beyond 2015 -16. The state-wise details \nare at Annexure-I and II. \n \n(b): Government has taken various steps for generating employment in the country \nlike encouraging private sector of economy,  fast tracking various projects involving \nsubstantial investment and increasing public expenditure on schemes like Prime \nMinister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP) run by Ministry of Micro, Small \n& Medium Enterprises, Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee \nScheme (MGNREGA), Pt. Deen  Dayal Upadhyaya Grameen Kaushalya Yojana (DDU-\nGKY)-National Rural Livelihoods Mission (NRLM) scheme run by Ministry of Rural \nDevelopment and National Urban Livelihoods Mission (NULM) run by Ministry of \nHousing & Urban Poverty Alleviation. The state wise details of achievements made \nunder these schemes are at Annexure-III, IV, V and VI.  \n \n(c): To ascertain employment and unemployment situation in the country, National \nSample Survey Office (NSSO), Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation \n(MoSPI) conducts labour force surveys on employmen t and unemployment in the \ncountry. The last such survey by NSSO was conducted during 2011 -12 and as per the \nsurvey results the unemployment rate on usual status basis was 2.2%. To measure \nchanges in the labour market on more frequent basis, MoSPI has launc hed Periodic \nLabour Force Survey (PLFS) from April, 2017. The PLFS aims to provide quarterly \nYear 2012-13 2013-14 2015-16 \nUnemployment Rate  4.0 % 3.4 % 3.7 % \nWorker Population \nRatio 51.0 % 53.7 % 50.5 % \n\nchanges of various indicators of the labour market in the urban areas and the annual \nestimates of different labour force indicators for both rural and urban areas,  at \nState/UT and all India level. In addition, Labour Bureau, Ministry of Labour and \nEmployment has conducted Employment-Unemployment Surveys annually and as per \nthe survey results of 2015-16 the unemployment rate on usual status basis for persons \naged 15 years and above was 3.7%. \n \n(d): Employment generation is a key priority of the Government. Government has \nfocused on making employment opportunities accessible to unemployed persons. In \norder to improve the employability of youth, around 22 Ministries/Departments run skill \ndevelopment schemes across various  sectors. Government announced a booster \npackage of Rs 6000 crores for the textile sector which is an employment intensive \nsector. \nPradhan Mantri Kaushal VikasYojana (PMKVY) is the flagship scheme of the Ministry \nof Skill Development & Entrepreneurship (MSDE). The objective of this Skill \nCertification Scheme is to enable a large number of Indian youth to take up industry -\nrelevant skill training that will help them in securing a better livelihood. Individua ls \nwith prior learning experience or skills will also be assessed and certified under \nRecognition of Prior Learning (RPL). \nGovernment is also implementing the National Career Service (NCS) Project which \ncomprises a digital portal that provides a nation -wide online platform for jobseekers \nand employers for job matching in a dynamic, efficient and responsive manner and has \na repository of career content.  \nA new scheme Pradhan MantriRojgarProtsahanYojna has been launched in the year \n2016-17 for incentivising i ndustry for promoting employment generation. Under this \nscheme employers would be provided and incentive t o enhance employment where \nthe Government will pay the employer’s contribution of 8.33% EPS made for the new \nemployees. In textiles (apparel) sector, the Government will also pay the 3.67% EPF \ncontribution of employers in addition to paying the 8.33%EPS contribution. \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nAnnex referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok  Sabha unstarred Question \nNo. 352 due for reply on 18.12.2017 \nUnemployment Rate for persons aged 15 years & above on usual status basis.   \n