    "ls|LS16_S13_U_457": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n  \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †457 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18th DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nColleges under RUSA \n \n†457.  SHRI OM PRAKASH YADAV: \n           SHRI NAGAR RODMAL: \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the total number of colleges  approved under the Rashtriya Uchchtar  Shiksha Abhiyan \n(RUSA) Scheme during the last three years and the current year, State/UT-wise; \n(b) the time by when these colleges were given approval in the State of Madhya Pradesh; \n(c) the details of financial allocation  made to establish above mentioned colleges,  State-\nwise; and \n(d) whether any college is proposed to be set up under RUSA Scheme in near future in the \ncountry including in the State of Bihar and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n \n(a) to (d): The proposals for setting up of new colleges under Rashtriya Uchchatar Shiksha \nAbhiyan (RUSA)  have to be included in the State Higher Education Plans (SHEPs) of the \nStates/UTs and are appraised by the Project Approval Board (PAB) of RUSA for approval. \nThe PAB, after appraisal of State Higher Education Plan (SHEP) submitted by resp ective \nState Governments/UTs has approved c entral assistance for creation of 72 new Model \nDegree Colleges (MDC s) in Educationally Backward Districts and 29 New Colleges \n(Professional & Technical).  \n \nThe PAB in its 7th meeting held on 28 th March, 2015 , inter-alia, has approved \ncentral assistance under RUSA for creation of five  new MDCs in Madhya Pradesh. The \nState-wise/UT-wise details of MDCs and New Colleges (Professional & Technical) \napproved under RUSA including  the State of Madhya Pradesh and Bihar are annexed. \n\nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 457 for \n18th December, 2017 asked by Shri Om Prakash Yadav  and Shri Nagar Rodmal , Hon’ble \nMembers of Parliament regarding Colleges under RUSA \nState No. of MDCs \nApproved \nunder RUSA \nAmount \nApproved \n(Rs. Cr) \nNo. of New Colleges \n(professional & \nTechnical) approved \nAmount \nApproved \n(Rs. Cr) \nAndhra Pradesh 6 72 - - \nArunachal Pradesh - - 1 26 \nAssam - - 3 78 \nBihar 7 84 - - \nChhattisgarh 5 60 - - \nGoa - - - - \nGujarat - - 2 52 \nHaryana - - - - \nHimachal Pradesh 2 24 1 26 \nJammu & Kashmir - - 2 52 \nJharkhand - - 3 78 \nKarnataka - - 1 26 \nKerala - - - - \nMadhya Pradesh 5 60 1 26 \nMaharashtra - - - - \nManipur - - 1 26 \nMeghalaya - - 2 52 \nMizoram - - 1 26 \nNagaland 1 12 - - \nOdisha 8 96 - - \nPunjab 2 24 2 52 \nRajasthan - - 2 52 \nSikkim - - 1 26 \nTamil Nadu - - 2 52 \nTelangana 4 48 - - \nTripura 4 45.399 - - \nUttar Pradesh 26 312 2 52 \nUttarakhand 1 10.29 - - \nWest Bengal - - 1 26 \nAndaman and Nicobar Islands 1 12 1 26 \nChandigarh - - - - \nDelhi - - - - \nDadra & Nagar Haveli - - - - \nDaman And Diu - - - - \nPuducherry - - - - \n 72 859.689 29 754 \n \n**** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_458": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.458 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nWORKFORCE GROWTH \n \n458.   SHRI MD. BADARUDDOZA KHAN: \nSHRI MOHD. SALIM: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the growth of India’s workforce in the last five years, year -wise, State/UT -wise \nand in absolute and percentage terms; \n(b) whether the growth in employment opportunities is not in proportion to t he \ngrowth in workforce during the said period; \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n(d) whether unemployment rate has gradually gone up in the country during the \nabove period; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof, sector -wise and the efforts being made by the \nGovernment to arrest this situation? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) to (d): As per the result of surveys on employment -unemployment conducted by \nLabour Bureau, Ministry of Labour and Employment, the estimated worker population \nratio and unemployment rate for persons aged 15 years and above on usual status basis \nin the country  is given below: \nYear Worker Population Ratio (in %) Unemployment Rate (in %) \n2011-12 53.6 3.3 \n2012-13 51.0 4.0 \n2013-14 53.7 3.4 \n2015-16 50.5 3.7 \n \nThe state-wise detail of worker population ratio is given at Annexure. \n \n\n \n(e): Employment generation is a key priority of the Government with focus on making \nemployment opportunities accessible to unemployed persons.  Government is \nimplementing schemes like Make -in-India, Digital India, Skill India, Swachch Bharat, \nStart-up India, S mart City projects etc., which will be creating more employment \nopportunities for the job seekers.  A booster package of Rs. 6000 crores for the textile \nsector which is an employment intensive sector has been announced. \n \n In order to improve the employabil ity of youth, around 22 Ministries/Departments \nrun skill development schemes across various sectors.  According to the data compiled \nby National Skill Development Agency (NSDA), Ministry of Skill Development and \nEntrepreneurship, 1.04 crore numbers of pers ons were skilled across various sectors \nduring 2015-16. \n \n Government has implemented the National Career Service (NCS) Project which \ncomprises a digital portal that provides a nation -wide online platform for the job \nseekers and employers for job -matching in a dynamic, efficient and responsive manner \nand has a repository of career content. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a to d) of the Lok  Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo. 458 due for reply on 18-12-2017. \n \nWorker Population Ratio for persons aged 15 years & above according to Usual \nPrincipal & Subsidiary Status Approach  \n(in %) \nSl. No. State/UTs 2011-12 2012-13 2013-14 2015-16 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 64.4 60.6 64.8 61.6 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 54.7 58.4 63.4 62.1 \n3 Assam 55.0 54.5 59.3 50.6 \n4 Bihar 49.0 44.1 48.0 48.4 \n5 Chhattisgarh 68.3 61.9 65.6 67.3 \n6 Delhi 45.3 41.8 40.2 40.8 \n7 Goa 43.0 43.7 47.9 44.7 \n8 Gujarat 54.6 48.1 52.9 49.0 \n9 Haryana 43.4 44.3 45.5 44.7 \n10 Himachal Pradesh 71.3 67.0 68.4 40.8 \n11 Jammu & Kashmir 44.0 42.5 43.3 36.7 \n12 Jharkhand 60.7 53.9 64.8 65.2 \n13 Karnataka 57.7 55.1 56.8 55.5 \n14 Kerala 44.7 40.6 48.0 45.2 \n15 Madhya Pradesh 57.4 57.5 59.2 44.8 \n16 Maharashtra 57.3 53.4 55.2 52.2 \n17 Manipur 62.4 54.7 61.2 59.9 \n18 Meghalaya 68.0 58.9 68.7 62.8 \n19 Mizoram 64.4 63.7 71.2 67.4 \n20 Nagaland 59.2 48.1 49.8 63.5 \n21 Odisha 52.2 52.3 54.0 51.2 \n22 Punjab 42.6 43.3 41.1 40.2 \n23 Rajasthan 53.1 51.2 54.5 53.7 \n24 Sikkim 62.6 57.8 64.8 61.4 \n25 Tamil Nadu 59.2 55.4 58.3 56.3 \n26 Telangana - - 65.1 56.6 \n27 Tripura 56.3 53.6 54.9 61.9 \n28 Uttarakhand 45.1 44.7 46.9 44.6 \n29 Uttar Pradesh 46.3 45.5 48.1 43.7 \n30 West Bengal 51.1 50.3 48.7 50.7 \n31 A & N Islands 51.5 37.6 53.7 54.1 \n32 Chandigarh 44.2 39.0 39.7 37.1 \n33 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 60.5 48.2 42.1 45.4 \n34 Daman & Diu 50.3 53.3 43.2 50.1 \n35 Lakshadweep 36.8 40.0 42.8 34.6 \n36 Puducherry 52.1 46.4 44.2 50.9 \n All India 53.6 51.0 53.7 50.5 \nSource: E&U Surveys  of Labour Bureau \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_459": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n DEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 459  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \n \nVacancies of Teachers in Higher Education \n \n \n459. SHRI MOHITE PATIL VIJAYSINH SHANKARRAO:  \n SHRI SHARAD TRIPATHI: \nDR. J. JAYAVARDHAN:  \nSHRI K. ASHOK KUMAR:  \nSHRI SATAV RAJEEV:  \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:  \nSHRI P.R. SUNDARAM: \nSHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL:  \nSHRI  DHANANJAY  MAHADIK:  \nDR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GAVIT: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether there is large number of teachers vacancies in Centrally funded institutions \nand universities in the country and if so, the details thereof State/UT -wise including \nKarnataka and the reasons therefor, institution-wise; \n(b) whether th e Government is considering bringing back retired teachers with good \nteaching record to fill in the large number of such vacancies and if so, the details \nthereof and the objectives behind the move; \n(c) whether the Government is also considering to extend teach ing assignment to good \nresearch scholars from reputed foreign universities and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the other steps taken by the Government to improve quality of education and research \nin Centrally funded institutions and universities? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n \n(a)  Madam. C ertain faculty positions are unfulfilled due to non -availability of suitable \ncandidates in premier institutions like IITs, these institutions make intense effort s to fill up \nthe vacancies through rolling advertisement process. In NITs, a massive recruitment drive \nis currently going on to fill up vacancies of about 1900 faculty positions . The teaching \nprogramme in these institutions however is supported by research  scholars, contract, \nadjunct and visiting faculty. As such, the teaching process is not adversely affected and \nthese institutions maintain high academic standards. The State -wise details of the \n\nvacancies of faculty positions would be collected and furnished.   \n \n(b) There is already a provision that teachers can be re -employed on contract appointment \nbeyond the age of sixty five years up  to the age of seventy years purely on the basis of \nmerit, experience, area of specialization and peer group review and only ag ainst available \nvacant positions. This is being used where needed.  \n \n(c) Institutions of National Importance have the option of selecting the research scholars from \nreputed foreign universities for teaching positions after taking due permissions from the \nMinistry of Home Affairs. Apart from this, the Department of Science and Technology is \noperating a scheme called ‘Visiting Advanced Joint Research’ for long -term teaching \nassignments by academicians from reputed foreign universities.  \n \n(d) In order to improve quali ty of education and research in Centrally Funded Institutions and \nuniversities, Government of India has introduced various schemes like Uchhatar Avishkar \nYojana (UAY), Impacting Research, Innovation and Technology (IMPRINT) and setting \nup of Research Parks  in IITs. Further, following steps have also been taken in Central \nuniversities: \ni) Introduction of Semester System. \nii) Periodic updation of curriculum. \niii) Introduction of choice-based credit system. \niv) Prescription of minimum qualification for appointment of teachers and other \nacademic staff. \nv) Introduction of mandatory assessment and accreditation. \nvi) Establishment of Internal Quality Assurance Cell. \nvii) Introduction of Faculty Recharge programme to augment research and teaching \nresources. \n \n**** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_460": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 460 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \nMINIMUM WAGES \n460. SHRIMATI SANTOSH AHLAWAT: \n        SHRI RAM CHARAN BOHRA: \n        SHRIMATI RITI PATHAK:  \n        SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARSWATI:  \n        SHRI R. DHRUVA NARAYANA:  \n        SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY:  \n        SHRI BADRUDDIN AJMAL: \n  \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a)whether minimum wages fixed for various types of unskilled lab ourers \nincluding those working in farming and non -farming sector under the \nMinimum Wages Act, 1948 vary amongst States;  \n(b)if so, the details thereof, State/ UT -wise along with the reasons for \ndifferential wages;  \n(c)whether the Government has amended/pro poses to amend the Minimum \nWages Act, 1948 and formulate a uniform wage policy for the entire \ncountry and if so, the details and the salient features thereof;  \n(d)whether the Government has received feedbacks from the States and \nother stakeholders in this regard and if so, the details thereof along with \nthe followup action taken by the Government thereon; and  \n(e)the other steps taken/being taken by the Government f or welfare and \nbetterment of labourers in the country? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) & (b): Under the provisions of the Minimum Wages Act, 1948, both \nCentral and State Governments are appropriate  Governments to fix, review \nand revise the minimum wages of the workers employed in the scheduled \nemployment including farming and non -farming sector under their respective \njurisdictions. \nContd..2/- \n  \n\n \n::  2  :: \n \nMinimum wages in States vary due to variati ons in socio -\neconomic and agro -climatic conditions, income, prices of essential \ncommodities, paying capacity, productivity and local conditions. A statement \nshowing the Category wise variation in minimum wages in different \nStates/Union Territories as on 01.11.2017 is at Annexure. \n \n(c) & (d): Proposal to amend the Minimum Wages Act, 1948 is not being \npursued by the Government presently. In the mean time, the Code on Wages \nhas been introduced in Lok Sabha on 10.08.2017 and which, inter -alia, has \nprovisions of the Minimum Wages Act, 1948. \n(e):   The labour welfare measures recently initiated for betterment of \nlabour force include minimum pension of Rs.1000/ -per month to the \npensioners under Employees’ Pension Scheme (EPS), 1995, portability of \nprovident fund ac count, National Career Service portal, Employees State \nInsurance Corporation 2.0(Health Reforms of ESIC), Revision in eligibility and \ncalculation ceiling under the Payment of Bonus Act, 1965 etc. \n \n*                                                     ******* \n  \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure in reply to part (a) & (b) of Lok Sabha unstarred question No. 460 for 18.12.2017  \nCategory wise variation in Minimum Wages per day in All States as on 01.11.2017 (Provisional)  \nSl. \nNo. State/UT \nCategories \nUnskilled Semi Skilled Skilled Highly Skilled \nMin. Max. Min. Max. Min. Max. Min. Max. \n Central Sphere 308.00 536.00 315.00 593.00 342.00 653.00 373.00 653.00 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 69.27 - - - - - - 895.83 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh  150.00 170.00 160.00 180.00 170.00 190.00 - - \n3 Assam* 240.00 - 280.00 - 350.00 - 450.00 - \n4 Bihar 181.00 197.00 188.00 206.00 232.00 251.00 282.00 308.00 \n5 Chhattisgarh 234.00 325.00 249.95 350.00 242.79 380.00 338.00 410.00 \n6 Goa 307.00 465.00 321.00 465.00 386.00 465.00 418.00 465.00 \n7 Gujarat 178.00 276.00 276.00 284.00 284.00 293.00 - - \n8 Haryana 318.46 - 334.39 351.11 368.66 387.10 406.45 - \n9 Himachal Pradesh 184.87 210.00 199.12 227.00 228.37 254.17 242.40 314.50 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 225.00 - 350.00 - 400.00 - - - \n11 Jharkhand  221.61 237.44 232.16 253.27 306.03 327.14 353.52 369.90 \n12 Karnataka 231.92 569.44 272.13 476