    "ls|LS16_S13_U_468": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 468 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.12.2017 \n \nSUB-CATEGORISATION OF SCS  \n \n468. SHRI NANDI YELLAIAH:  \n \nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has decided to take opinion from all the State Governments and \nUnion Territories regarding sub -categorization of Scheduled Castes into A, B, C, D sub -groups \nand if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) the details of opinion sought and opinion given by the States, State-wise; and  \n \n(c) the action being taken by the Government in this regard as the matter is pending since several \nyears? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \n( VIJAY SAMPLA) \n(a) to (c): National Commission to examine the issue of Sub -categorisation of Scheduled Castes \nin Andhra Pradesh (NCSCSC) has recommended amendment of Article 341 of the Constitution \nto provide for sub -categorisation and de -sub-categorisation of Scheduled Castes. View s of the \nmajor stake holders viz. the State Governments and Union Territory Administrations on the \nrecommendation of the NCSCSC has been sought. Process of consultation has not concluded. \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_469": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 469 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19th DECEMBER, 2017 /AGRAHAYANA 28, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nWAIVING OF DEPLOYMENT CHARGES \n \n469.   SHRIMATI PRATYUSHA RAJESHWARI SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the State Government of  Odisha has requested for waiving of \ncharges towards deployment of CRPF in the State for anti-Naxal operation; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the response of the Government thereto; and  \n \n(c)  the time by which the said request is likely to be approved? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n(a) to (c):  A request was received from Govt. of Odisha requesting for a complete \nwaiver of charges towards deployment of the Central Forces in Odisha for \ntackling Left Wing Extremism. The request of the State Government of Odisha \nwas examined and the State Government has been info rmed that as per extent \npolicy of the Government, all States, except the fe w special category States viz; \nNorth Eastern State s, Jammu & Kashmir and Himachal Pradesh, are required to \nbear full charges for deployment of Central Armed Police Forces (CAPFs) in  their \nStates. Since Central Government is incurring huge expenditure on deployment of \nCAPFs in the States and due to overall constraint of resources, it is not feasible \nto exempt the State Government from payment of deployment charges.  \n****** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_470": " \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 470 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.12.2017 \n \nINTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ON CONSUMER PROTECTION \n \n470. SHRI DHANANJAY MAHADIK: SHRI ASADUDDIN OWAISI: SHRIMATI SUPRIYA \nSULE: DR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GAVIT: DR. J. JAYAVARDHAN: SHRI P.R. \nSUNDARAM: SHRI MOHITE PATIL VIJAYSINH SHANKARRAO:  \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk/k vkSj lkoZtfud foRrj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has organised the First International Conference on Consumer \nProtection recently and if so, the details thereof along with the theme of the Conference;  \n(b) the number of Indian and International delegates who had attended the conference;  \n(c) the main issues discussed in the conference along with the outcome thereof;  \n(d) whether the Government will bring in a new law to crack down on misleading advertise ments \nand set up a Central authority to fast track redressal of consumer grievances and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(e) whether the existing Consumer Protection Act is not sufficient to deal with such misleading \nadvertisements, if so, the reasons therefor and the further steps taken or being taken by the \nGovernment in this regard; and  \n(f)  the other steps taken by the Government to safeguard the interest of consumers? \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k jkT; ea=h  \n¼Jh lh- vkj- pkS/kjh½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to (c) : Yes, Madam. An international conference on consumer protection was held on 26 -27 \nOctober, 2017 in New Delhi in association with UNCTAD having participation from countries in the \nEast, South and South -East Asia with the theme “Empowering Consumers in New Markets”. Arou nd \n1800 delegates including around 60 foreign delegates participated in the conference. The main issues \ndiscussed were United Nations Guidelines for Consumer Protection and their Implementation; \nStakeholder Participation in Consumer Protection; Protection of Online Consumers; Fostering Consumer \nInclusion in Financial Services; Consumer Education and Empowerment;  and Special Challenges in \nProtecting Vulnerable and Economically Disadvantaged Consumers . The Conference inter -alia drew \nconclusions such as - comprehensive implementation of the United Nations Guidelines for Consumer \nProtection as a priority for Governments and stakeholders in ensuring more effective and better -\ncoordinated protection efforts in all countries and across all areas of commerce; Protect ion of \nconsumers’ rights in the digital context as a key for a sustainable and inclusive development of e -\ncommerce, which also needs to address cross-border cooperation and enforcement etc. \n \n(d) to (f) : Under the provisions of the existing Consumer Protection Act, 1986, a Consumer Forum \nwill  take action only after a complaint is filed by a consumer. The Government has already introduced \nthe Consumer Protection Bill, 2015 in Parliament which seeks to esta blish an executive agency to be \nknown as the Central Consumer Protection Authority which would look into matters relating to violation \nof rights of consumers, unfair trade practices and false or misleading advertisements which are \nprejudicial to the intere sts of public and consumers and to promote, protect and enforce the rights of \nconsumers as a class.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_471": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE,\n \nCOOPERATION\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n4\n71\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \nTHE \n19\nTH \n \nDECEMBER\n, \n201\n7\n \n \nCULTIVATION OF HT VARIETY OF COTTON\n \n \n471. \n \nSHRI\n \nR. DHRUVA\n \nNARAYANA:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n  \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether there is illegal sale and\n \ncultivation of Herbicide Tolerant (HT)\n \nvariety of cotton \nsince 2008 without\n \nstatutory approval of GEAC in the\n \ncountry;\n \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhethe\nr the Government has taken\n \nnote of this practice and appropriate\n \nmeasures have \nbeen taken to control the\n \nsame in the country; and\n \n \n(d) \n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \n\u0000कसा\nन \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(SHRI \nPARSHOTTAM  RUPALA\n)\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nNo, Madam.  None of the State Government has reported about the illegal sale \nand cultivation of Herbicide Tolerant (HT) cotton since 2008.  Only states of Andhra \nPradesh, \nTelangan\na & Maharashtra have reported traces of unapproved Herbicide Tolerant (HT) cotton \nin some of the farmers field during 2017\n-\n18.\n \n \n(c) & (d):\n \nYes, Madam.  Department of Agriculture, Cooperation and Farmers\n \nWelfare \n(DAC&FW\n) has issued advisory to all concern c\notton growing \nstates to take all possible \nmeasures as per law to curb and cont\nr\nol the illegal sale and cultivation of Herbicide Tolerant \n(HT) variety.\n \n \n \n************\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_472": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMNAL HUSBANDRY, DAIRYING AND FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.472 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nAMENDMENT IN CRZ NORMS \n \n472.  SHRI K.C VENUGOPAL: \n \n     Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE कृषि और ककसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken any measures to remove the concerns of fishermen \ncommunity regarding Coastal Regulation Zone (CRZ) norms; \n \n(b)  if s o, the measures taken by the Government to remove hardships in constructing fishing \ninfrastructure in CRZ regions; and  \n \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to amend the CRZ norms to remove the hardships of \nfishermen and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \n(SHRIMATI  KRISHNA RAJ) \n \n(a) Yes, Madam.  \n \n(b) The MoEF & CC stated in their letter dated 07 th November 2013 that   the CRZ \nNotification, 2011 permits fishery related infrastructure facilities required for local fishing \ncommunities such as fish drying yards, auction halls, net mending yards, traditional boat building \nyards, ice plants, ice crushing  units, and fish curing facilities in the CRZ areas. Fish processing, \nwhich is prohibited in the CRZ, could come up beyond 500 m of the High Tide Line (HTL). \n \n(c) Ministry of Environment and Forest (MoEF & CC) mentioned that suggestions and \nproposals have been received from various stakeholders of coastal States/UTs for review of CRZ \nNotification, 2011 for development of infrastructure along the coast, including concerns of fishing \ncommunity. These have been deliberated upon by a Committee constituted by th e Ministry of \nEnvironment, Forest and Climate Change, for a comprehensive review of the CRZ Notification. \n \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_473": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEAUTICALS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 473 \n TO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH December, 2017  \n \nNIPER in Punjab \n \n473. SHRI RAVNEET SINGH:  \n \n Will the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is considering proposals for improvement/ upgradation of \nthe National Institute of Pharmaceutical Education and Research (NIPER), Mohali in \nPunjab;  \n \n(b) wheth er the Government has instituted any agreements for transfer of knowledge \nand technology between international institutes and NIPER and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Government is planning to upgrade the equipment and machinery in \nNIPER and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(d) the details of various upgrades that have taken place and funds allocated by the \nGovernment for development of NIPER as Model Pharmaceutical Research \nOrganisation during the last three years? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS; \nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING AND MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS (SHRI \nMANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) Madam, The government had approved Rs. 249.455cr. during the year2015 -16 for \nimprovement /upgradation of National Institute of Pharmaceutical Education and \nResearch (NIPER), Mohali. \n \n(b) The Institute has signed research and academic exchange agreements with \ninstitutions / universities of various countries including those from Egypt, Japan, USA \netc. \n \n(c) and (d) The government has already approved proposal for upgradation of the \nInstitute and released Rs. 9.79 cr during the financial year 2015 -16. However, the \nInstitute has not been able to utilize the amount.  \n \n****** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_474": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF CHEMICALS AND PETROCHEMICALS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  474 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.12.2017 \nPLASTIC PARKS \n***** \n \n474. SHRI DUSHYANT CHAUTALA \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n \na) whether the Government has decided for setting up of Plastic Parks and proposed to \nimplement the same during 12th and 13th Five Year Plan period; \n \nb) if so, the details thereof and the total number of plastic parks established by the Government \nduring the 12th Plan till now and proposed under 13th Plan; \n \nc) whether the Government has set up any monitoring mechanism to ensure the functio ning of \nplastic parks in an eco-friendly manner and production of only recyclable plastics; and \n \nd)  if so, the details thereof and the steps taken/ being taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE FOR MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS (RAO INDERJIT \nSINGH) \n(a) & (b) The Scheme for setting up of Plastic Parks approved for implementation for the 12 th \nplan and beyond, till 2019 -20, provides for central funding up to 50% of the project cost, \nsubject to a ceiling of Rs. 40.00 crore for setting up of state -of-the-art infrastructure  and \nenabling common facilities per Plastic Park, based on the project proposal received from the \nState Government. The remaining project cost is funded by the State Government or its \nagencies. Out of 10 Plastic Parks approved , 4 Plastic Parks are in various stages of \nimplementation in the States of Assam, Madhya Pradesh, Odisha & Tamil Nadu. \n \n(b) & (d) Under the Plastic Park Scheme Guidelines, the final approval is accorded to the \nDetailed Project Report (DPR) proposal received from the State Government subject to the \nstatutory approvals and environmental clearances. The Scheme also provides for building \ninfrastructure to support plastic production units through establishment of effluent treatment \nplant, hazardous waste management, incinerator, buildings and equipment / machinery for \ncommon facilities for plastic recycling etc. as well as adherence to the existing rules & norms \nby units set up in the Plastic Parks. \n \n**** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_475": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 475 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19th DECEMBER, 2017 /AGRAHAYANA 28, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nCOMPENSATION FOR JAWANS \n \n475.   SHRI G. HARI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that at present  the compensation from all sources,  \nCentre and State Governments and ex-gratia from the force comes to around Rs. \n60 lakh for the Jawans who died in action; \n \n(b) if so, whether the Government is  considering to enhance the compensation  \nfrom all sources to Rs. 1.0 crore; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n(a) to (c):  The next of Kins (NOKs) of the Central Armed Police Force (CAPF) & \nAssam Rifles (AR) personnel killed in action are entitled to get Central ex -gratia \ncompensation which has recently been enhanced to Rs. 35 lac as per Department \nof Pensions and Pension’s We lfare (DoP&PW) OM dated 04/08/2016. Many \nStates/UTs (where such incidents occur and Home State/UT of the CAPFs \npersonnel killed in action) also provide ex -gratia compensation which varies from \nState to State. The Central Government has also requested all t he States/UTs to \nexplore the possibility of enhancing financial component of their support. In \naddition to above, CAPFs & AR have their own welfare schemes and funds which \nare raised at force level to look after the needs of personnel and their families.  \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_476": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 476  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nQUANTITY OF RICE RELEASED TO KERALA \n \n476.  SHRIMATI P.K. SREEMATHI TEACHER: \nSHRI P. KARUNAKARAN: \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the quantum of rice released to Kerala under BPL, APL and AAY \nthrough the Public Distribution System (PDS) during the last three years; \n \n(b)  the details of wheat and rice released under various scheme for the \nlast three years; \n \n(c)  whether the distribution of Rice to Kerala State is pending and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n \n(d)  whether the Government has  received any representation from the \nState of Kerala regarding more allocation of  food grains and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n \n(e)  the steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR  CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n(a) to (e):    Kerala implemented National Food Security Act, 2013 with effect \nfrom 1-Nov-2016.    Allocation of foodgrain to Kerala under TPDS and under \nNFSA is as given below: \nS.No. Year Normal TPDS/NFSA Foodgrain allocation (Qty. \nMT) \n1. 2014-15 – TPDS  12,26,724 (that includes 10,01,844 MT of rice and \n2,24,880 MT of wheat) \n2. 2015-16 – TPDS 12,26,724 (that includes 10,01,844 MT of rice and \n2,24,880 MT of wheat) \n3. 2016-17 – TPDS \n(April-Oct) \n7,15,589 (that includes 05,84,409 MT of rice and \n1,31,180 MT of wheat) (proportionate of \n12,26,724 per annum for 7 months) \n4. 2016-17 – NFSA \n(Nov’16-\nMarch’17) \n5,93,770.415 MT (that includes 4,78,578.960 MT \nof rice and 1,15,191.455 MT of wheat)  \n(proportionate of 14,25,048.996 per annum for 5 \nmonths) \n...2/-  \n \n\n \n...2... \n \nIn addition to the above, in view of surplus availability of foodgrains \nin the Central Pool, Government of India also made additional ad hoc  \nallocation of foodgrain to Kerala to the tune of 3,68,639 MT, 3,65,256 MT and \n91,314 MT in 2014- 15, 2015- 16 and 2016- 17 respectively (which includes \n3,44,945 MT, 2,70,480 MT and 67,620 MT of rice and 23,694 MT, 94,776 MT \nand 23,694 MT of wheat respectively during these three years). \n \nThe average annual offtake of foodgrains under erstwhile TPDS \n(which includes Addl. APL) during three years immediately prior to the NFSA \ncoming into force (2010- 11 to 2012 -13) in respect of Kerala was 14.25 lakh \ntons, which has completely been protected under NFSA.  This principle has \nuniformly been applied to all States/UTs under NFSA.   \n \nUnder erstwhile TPDS, only 2.50 lakh MT of foodgrains for AAY \nhouseholds was being allocated per year at Rs.2/3 per kg for wheat/rice, \nwhereas under NFSA, the annual allocation of 10.25 lakh tons (out of total \n14.25 lakh MT of foodgrain) for the coverage determined for the State is \nbeing allocated at Rs.2/3 per kg for wheat/rice, which results in higher \nsubsidy burden on Government of India and higher savings for the State \nGovernment/beneficiaries in Kerala. \n \nUnder Welfare Institution Scheme Kerala was allocated 2,136 MT, \n2,322 MT and 1,728 MT of foodgrain (wheat and rice) during 2013- 14, 2014-15 \nand 2015- 16 respectively.  The allocation under  this scheme is subject to \nsubmission of UCs and unspent balance of foodgrain for the previous \nallocations. \n \nRequests were received from the Government of Kerala for \nincreasing foodgrain allocation to the State under NFSA.  However, in view of \nthe uniform policy for all the States/UTs for determining foodgrain allocation \nunder the Act, this could not be agreed to.  Government of Kerala has further \nbeen informed that any additional requirement of foodgrain over and above \nentitlement under NFSA can be made at  economic cost.  The State \nGovernment can also participate in Open Market Sale Scheme (D) organized \nby FCI as the rate of foodgrain under OMSS (D) is less than that of economic \ncost. \n \n**** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_477": "O.I.H\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE,\n \nCOOPERATION\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n477\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \nTHE \n19\nTH \n \nDECEMBER\n, \n201\n7\n \n \nAGRICULTURAL PROJECTS\n \n \n477. \n \nSHRI ANSHUL VER\nMA:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n  \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe details of agricultural projects\n \nbeing funded by overseas and international\n \nagencies, \nState\n-\nwise including Karnataka;\n \n \n(b)\n \n \nthe present status of such\n \nagricultural projects, State\n-\nwise; and\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether any sort of foreign\n \nassistance has been sought by the States\n \nrecently for a new \nproject and if so, the\n \ndetails thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(SHRI\n \nPARSHOTTAM RUPALA\n)\n \n \n(a) \n&\n \n(b)\n:\n \n \nThe details of agricultural projects being funded by overseas and international \nagencies, State\n-\nwise including Karnataka are enclosed as per \nannexure\n-\n \nI \n \n \n(c)\n:\n \n \nAs per \nannexure\n-\nII\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSl. \nNo.\n Project Name\n Doner\nProject ID\nDate of \nApproval \nDate of \nClosing\nState / Central Project\nTotal \nFund \nSanctione\nd / Loan \nor Credit \nAmount\nCum. \nDisbursemen\nt \n1\nUttar Pradesh Sodic Lands Reclamation - Phase III \nProject\nWorld Bank\nP112033\n30-Jun-09\n29-Dec-18\nState (Uttar Pradesh)\n197.00\n153.2\n2\nMaharashtra Agricultural Competitiveness Project\nWorld Bank\nP120836\n28-Sep-10\n31-Oct-18\nState (Maharashtra)\n100.00\n67.2\n3\nRajasthan Agricultural Competitiveness Project\nWorld Bank\nP1