    "ls|LS16_S13_U_478": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 478 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.12.2017 \n \nDE-ADDICTION CAMPS  \n \n478. DR. BOORA NARSAIAH GOUD: \n \nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether de-addiction camps are being organized by the Government regularly;  \n \n(b) if so, the details and the number of beneficiaries from such camps during each of the last three  years and the \ncurrent year;  \n \n(c) the status of the National Toll free Drug de-Addiction Helpline; and  \n \n(d) the details of the number of victims of drug addiction in the country during the said period? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \n(SHIR VIJAY SAMPLA) \n(a) & (b): This Ministry implements a “Central Sector Scheme of Assistance for Prevention of Alcoholism an d \nSubstance (Drug) Abuse”. The Scheme has the provision of financial assistance to Non -Governmental \nOrganisations, Panchayati Raj Institutions, Urban Local  Bodies etc. for, inter -alia, running and maintenance of \nIntegrated Rehabilitation Centre for Addicts (IRCAs) and to provide composite/ integrated services f or the \nrehabilitation of the addicts. These IRCAs are responsible for Preventive Education and Awar eness Generation \nprogrammes in their neighbourhood, educational institutions, workplace and slums with the purpose of  \nsensitising the community about the impact of addiction and the need to take professional help for t reatment. \nTotal number of beneficiarie s under the scheme for t he year 2014 -15,2015-16,2016-17& 2017 -18(upto \n13.12.2017) are 108855, 146124, 114759 & 65682 respectively. \n(c): This Ministry has setup a National Toll Free Drug  de-addiction Helpline No. 1800 -11-0031 w.e.f. 7 th \nJanuary, 2015 to hel p the victims of drug abuse, their family & society at large. The helpline has become \noperational  24x7 since March, 2017. \n(d): The last National Survey on the extent, pattern and trend of drug abuse was sponsored by Minist ry of Social \nJustice and Empowerment and the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC) in the year 2000 -2001 \nand its report was published in 2004. It was estimated that about 732 lakh persons in India were use rs of alcohol \nand drugs. Of these 87 lakh used Cannabis, 20 lakh used opi ates and 625 lakh were users of Alcohol. However, \nthe sample size being small (40,697 males within the age group of 12 -16 years both in rural and urban areas) \nvis-à-vis the country’s population, the estimates can at best be taken as indicative only. \n Thereafter, no National Survey on drug abuse was con ducted. However, the Ministry ha s, in the month \nof August, 2016, assigned the work of conducting the National Survey on Extent and Pattern of Substa nce Use \nin India to National Drug Dependence Treatment Centre  (NDDTC), All India Institute of Medi cal Sciences \n(AIIMS), New Delhi.  The survey will provide national and State -level estimates of proportion and absolute \nnumber of people who use various substances and people who are suffering from substance use disorders. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_480": "     \n   GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \n \n   UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 480 \n                         TO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 19, 2017 \n \n                                                                                                                                                                         CITY COMPOST  \n \n \n \nNo. 480            SHRIMATI KIRRON KHER: \n      \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AN D URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n \n(a) whether the policy for promot ion of city compost has been \nsuccessful in creating a market  for compost and increasing compost \nconsumption in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) the details of employment oppo rtunities generated by city waste \ncomposting in urban areas; and \n \n(c) whether the Government has take n steps to regulate production of \ncompost according to the amount of compost cons umed by the market \nand if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n   \n       ANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY \nOF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a): Yes, Madam. T he Policy on Promotion of City Compost has been \nsuccessful in creating a market  for compost and increasing the \ncompost consumption.  The production of compost has increased by \n18% and its sale has increased by 29% since the launch of  policy in \nJanuary, 2016.  \n \n(b): Employment opportunities hav e been generated from city waste \ncomposting in urban areas, including the involvement of Self Help \nGroups (SHGs) in the collection and processing of waste. Precise \ndetails of employment formation in the informal sector are not \nmaintained.  \n \n(c): Government does not regulat e production of compost according to \nthe amount of compost consumed by the market. \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_481": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 481 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH DECEMBER, 2017 /AGRAHAYANA 28, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nPENDING BILL \n \n481.  SHRI JAYADEV GALLA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether it is true that the  Legislative Assembly of Andhra Pradesh  has \npassed The Andhra Pradesh Money  Lenders Bill, 2016 and sent it to the  \nGovernment for approval in 2016; and \n \n(b) if so, the reasons why the Bill is  still pending with the Government and the \ntime by which the Bill is likely to be approved? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) : Yes, Madam.  \n(b) : The Andhra Pradesh Money Lenders Bill, 2016 was received in the Ministry \nof Home Affairs  on 20.01.2016 for the consideration of the Hon’ble President of \nIndia under Article 201 of the Constitution of India. This Bill has been returned to \nthe State Government on their request on 20.04.2017. \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_482": "Annexure-A \nS. \nNo \nSubject Status of the complaint \n \n1 Complaint of corruption in IFFCO ref. \nreceived from Sh. Nishikant Dubey, \nMP (LS) through CVC. \nComplaint has been forwarded to CBI, \nED for investigation. Report is awaited. \n2 RCs against Shri Mahesh Mittal, \nDirector M/s Narmada Extension, \nIndore, against RCF - Complaint \nreceived from CVC pertaining to filing \nof chargesheet by CBI against Sh. \nMahesh Mittal and recommendation of \nRDA by CBI against officials of RCF.  \nCase in under investigation in \nconsultation with CVC and CVO, RCF. \n3. Irregularities in NFL’s contract for \nproduction of Urea and Ammonia. \nRef. received from CVC. \nReport has been sent to CVC and \nadvice of the Commission is awaited.  \n4. Complaint against Sh. Jaiveer \nSrivastava, Ex -CMD, FACT raising \nseveral matters such as promotions of \nofficers in FACT, travel bills of CMD, \nmissappropriation of funds for \ntransportatio, manupulation of \napplication sent to PESB, \nappointment of consutants etc –  \nReceived from Sh. T.N.Unnikrishnan \nReport has been sent to CVC and \nadvice of the Commission is awaited. \n5. Complaint against official of FACT  \npertaining to appointment of \nCOO/FEDO- Received from Sh. T.N. \nUnnikrishnan. \nReport has been sent to CVC and \nadvice of the Commission is awaited. \n6. Complaint against official of FACT  \npertaining to appointment of \nCEO/FRBL – Received from Sh. T.N. \nUnnikrishnan. \nCase in under investigation in \nconsultation with CVO, FACT. \n7. Complaint against officials of \nFAGMIL-Reg. recruitment post 2010 \nin FAGMIL rec eived from Sh. Harish, \nJodhpur. \nCase in under investigation in \nconsultation with CVO, FAGMIL. \n8. Complaint regarding tender for \nallotment of Kullu & Hamirpur out \nagencies in r/o NFL-  Received from \nDirector Traffic , Railway Board. \nCase in under investigat ion in \nDepartment. \n9. Complaint against Sh.  I. Vijaykumar, \nEx-CMD, MFL -Received from CVC \npertaining to appointment of Sh. Rajat \nSharma, Contract Labour in MFL. \nCase in under investigation in \nconsultation with CVC and CVO, MFL. \n10. Corruption and Irregularities in \ntenders for inviting bids for the ROM \nGypsum in four mines in FAGMIL- \nReceived from M/s Raj Gypsum \nUdyog Jaipur (Rajasthan.) \n \nCharge Sheet has been issued to Sh. \nS.K Das, Ex CMD vide Memorandum \ndated 27.11.2017.  \n11. Procurement of phosphoric Acid by \nMFL- Ref. received from CVO,MFL \nCase in under investigation in \nconsultation with CVO, MFL. \n\n12. Self contained note forwarded by CBI \nto this department regarding violations \nof tender norms while awarding civil \nengineering works in FACT and other \nanother related to irregularities in \nappointment of consultant in FEDO.  \nCase in under investigation in \nconsultation with CMD, FACT. \n13. Complaint against Ex -CMD, NFL and \nother officials of NFL raising several \nallegations such as irregu alarities in \nAGM Meeting in NFL, procurement of \nHDPE bags by NFL, promotons etc.: \nRef. received from CVC \nAll allegations have been closed in \nconsultation with CVC except \nallegation pertaining to the \nprocurement of HDPE bags which has \nbeen referred to CBI for detailed \ninvestigation . Report from CBI is \nawaited. \n14. Procurment of SSP on long term basis \nfrom M/ S Devyani Phosphates Pvt. \nLtd.by RCF. \nCase in under investigation in \nconsultation with CVC and CVO, RCF. \n15. Complaint against MFL officers in the \nmatter of procurement of Oil \nCompressors-Ref received from \nCVO,MFL \nCBI has filed Charge sheet in the \nSpecial court, Chennai. Copy of \nchargesheet furnished by CBI had \nbeen sent to the CVC on  22.11.2017  \nto seek its further advice in the matter \nwhich is awaited.  \n16. Complaint under public interest \ndisclosure per whistle Blower Policy \nMFL regarding procurment of  Primary \nReformer Tubes in MFL- Regarding. \nCBI has registered FIR in the matter. \nReport from CBI is awaited. \n17. Complaint against Sh. D. \nSundraShekran, GM, MFL- regarding \ntinkering of various tenders such as \nlabour contract, plant upkeep and \nmanagement, b agging contract in \norder to suit certain parties.  \nCharge Sheet  has been issued to  Dr. \nI. Vijayakumar Ex-CMD, MFL.  \n18. Regarding purchase of  Import \nDAP/NPK by RCF from Zuari Agro -\nChemicals Ltd.(ZACL) - Explanation \nsought by Hon’ble MOS(I/C),C&F) \nReport has been sent to CVC for 1 st \nstage advice. Advice of the CVC is \nawaited.  \n19. Appointment of wholesale dealers in \nNFL \n Chargehseet for Major Penalty \nProceedings has been served to Sh. P \nK Kaul, then Dir.(Mktg.), NFL. The \npenalty to be imposed on then CMD, \nNFL is under consideration of \nDepartment. \n20. Complaint against Sh. P.S. Ghalot, \nMD,IPL- Ref. from CVC \nCase in under investigation in \nconsultation with CVC. \n \n********** \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S13_U_483": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 483 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nWASTAGE OF FOODGRAINS \n \n483.  SHRI PR. SENTHIL NATHAN: \nSHRI BHARATHI MOHAN R.K.: \nSHRI M. MURALI MOHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken serious note of wastage of \nfoodgrains due to poor storage, pilferage, theft, rotting,  decaying etc. and if \nso, the details thereof  and the decision taken by the Government  in this \nregard; \n \n(b)  whether the Government has taken steps to increase the overall \nstorage capacities of foodgrains in the country  keeping in view the need to \nprocure and store more foodgrains in the coming years  and if so, the details \nthereof and the total  volume of foodgrains in Government‘s storage till date \nalong with the details of the capacity increase made in the last three years; \n \n(c)  the quantum of foodgrains in terms  of bags found unfit for human  \nconsumption, exclusively rice and wheat location-wise and State-wise during \nthe said period; \n \n(d)  the remedial measures being undertaken by the Government to \nprevent accumulation of spoiled foodgrains  exclusively rice and wheat in \nvarious godowns in the entire country when millions of people are facing \nstarvation deaths; and \n \n(e)  the corrective measures being undertaken to prevent the lapses in \nthe process of storage to distribution of rice and wheat? \n \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR  CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n(a) & (b) No damage of foodgrains have been reported due to lack of \nadequate storage and handling facilities  in Food Corporation of India (FCI). \nProcured foodgrains are stored in scientific godowns and code of scientific \nstorage practices are followed during storage. Inspite of all precautions, \nsome quantity of foodgrains may get damaged/become non- issuable during \nstorage due to various reasons such as storage pests’ attack, leakages in \ngodowns, exposure to rains, floods, etc. A quantity of 3115 tons and 8776 \ntons of foodgrai ns accrued as damaged/non- issuable in FCI godowns during \n2015-16 and 2016-17 respectively. \n...2/- \n\n...2... \n \n         Sufficient storage capacity is available for storage of Central Pool \nfoodgrains stocks. Storage capacity for Central Pool foodgrain is 728.83 lakh \ntons against the stock of 378.77 lakh tons as on 31.10.2017.  \n \n         Total volume of Central Pool  foodgrains is 347.91 lakh tons as on \n01.12.2017. Following steps have been taken by the Government to augment \ncovered storage capacity: \n \n1.  Government has formulated Private Entrepreneurs Guarantee (PEG) \nScheme for construction of godowns for storage of foodgrains by private \nparties, Central Warehouse Corporation (CWC), State Warehouse \nCorporations (SWCs) and other State agencies. Storage capacity of 139.72 \nlakh tons has been completed as on 31.10.2017.  \n \n2.  In addition, a capacity of 1,22,270 tons in North Eastern States and \n20,000 tons in other than NE States as on 30.11.2017 has been completed \nunder 12\nth Five Year Plan.  \n \n3.  Government has also approved action plan for construction of steel \nSilos for a capacity of 100 lakh  tons in phased manner. \n \n          Details of the capacity added during the last three years is as under: \n(fig in tons) \nYear Capacity under PEG Scheme Capacity under Plan Scheme \n2014-15 830000 43480 \n2015-16 646000 64810 \n2016-17 383000 3980 \nTotal 1859000 112270 \n \n         The remedial measures taken by the Government to prevent losses on \naccount of pilferage and theft are at Annexure-I.   \n \n(c)   Location wise/bag wise/State wise details of the foodgrains accrued \nas damaged/non-issuable in FCI during the last three years and current year \nare at Annexure-II.     \n \n(d) & (e) Steps taken by the Government to avoid the damage in storage to \ndistribution of foodgrains are at Annexure-III. \n**** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( a) & (b) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO 483 TO BE ANSWERED ON 19 TH DECEMBER, 2017 IN LOK \nSABHA. \nREMEDIAL MEASURES TAKEN BY THE GOVERNMENT TO PREVENT LOSSES \nON ACCOUNT OF PILFERAGE AND THEFT. \nFollowing measures were taken by FCI to avoid foodgrains losses on account of \npilferage and theft.   \n1. Installation of barbed wires fencing of boundary walls and provision of high mast \nlights in the godowns/complexes. \n2. Deployment of Security staff of FCI as well as other agencies    like Home Guards, \nDGR sponsored agency & Special Police Officers and  state armed police on need \nbasis. \n3. Security Inspections as well as surprise checks of the depots are conducted from time \nto time at various levels to detect and plug any security lapses.  \n4. Lodging of FIRs with the Police where pilferage and theft has come to notice.  Apart \nfrom this, departmental action including recovery of loss has been resorted to as a \ndeterrent measure.  \n5. Apart from the above, monitoring by installing CCTV camera systems and other \ngadgets is also being done as CCTV Cameras are being installed in all FCI owned \nDepots. CCTV cameras have been installed in 496 FCI depots across the country and \ninstallation is in progress in 17 more depots. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE- II \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO \n483 TO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH DECEMBER, 2017 IN LOK SABHA. \nLOCATION WISE/BAG WISE/STATE WISE DETAILS OF THE FOODGRAINS \nACCRUED AS DAMAGED/NON-ISSUABLE IN FCI DURING LAST THREE YEARS AND \nCURRENT YEAR  \n                            (A)  2017-18 AS ON 1.11.2017  \n       (Figs. in MTs) \nZONE REGION